From 5442c02d0cc40650a4fd35182676c383802290cc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 15:13:17 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] Voices: a phone call, Siri or another app taking the
 sound pauses the voice at its word

Build 95 note 25, the arbiter's brief for Updates 17, item 4. Nothing a reader
sees yet (no screen speaks with the phone's voice until the picker is his yes),
but the engine now behaves as the recordings already do.

- Android (PurifySpeechPlugin.java): while a text is read the app holds the
  audio focus for media speech, as a player does. A moment's loss (a call, the
  assistant, an alarm) stops the voice and says so; the focus coming back says
  the reading may go on. A loss for good (another app keeps the sound) lets the
  focus go and leaves the reading paused for the reader. Not over a call: a
  reading asked for during one waits, paused. The focus is let go when the
  reader stops it and when the reading ends. A sound that only asks the others
  to go quieter (a notification, a navigation prompt) is left to the phone. The
  ambience is the web view's Web Audio, which holds no focus, so it goes on
  under the voice as before.
- iPhone (PurifySpeechPlugin.swift): the audio session's interruption stops a
  reading at its word and says so; its end says whether the phone lets the
  reading go on (shouldResume, a call or Siri over).
- The session (lib/listen/speech/session.ts) pauses the player at the word on
  "interruption", without asking the phone to stop again, and takes it up there
  when the phone gives the sound back and says it may; otherwise it waits for
  Play. A reader's own Pause during the call wins, and lets the claim go.

Tests that fail on the old code: 3 of 5 new in session.test.ts; the plugin
contract in nativeSpeech.test.ts now holds both halves to it (it held Android to
asking no focus, which is what let the voice talk on through a call). The Java
was compiled here against stand-ins for the Android and Capacitor classes; the
Swift is proved by the iOS build, and both by the phone walk.

